      "a": "Summary\nSummary: delete a card. A Delete button with data-delete. On click, read the card id, filter it out of the array, then showAll again. The DOM matches the array. This is a hands-on lab, not a riddle. You should be able to explain the function out loud, then type it from memory. What the code is doing: Walk the snippet like this: `cards = cards.filter((c) => c.id !== id` — Delete in data. `showAll(cards` — Delete on the page. `btn.dataset.delete = \"1\"` — mark as the delete control. A line you can point at: grid.addEventListener(\"click\", function (e) { In an interview, start with the job in plain words (the first sentences here), then walk the snippet. Do not start with a shop story. When you are done: run it, break it with empty input or a wrong id, then fix the check. That is the whole point of a practice row. Add/delete/search stay in one paint function. Memory does not leak per-button listeners. User text cannot run as code.\n\nThe problem before\nPeople typed twenty cards in HTML. New cards did not get click listeners. innerHTML with user text became XSS. The page reloaded on every submit. This lab exists because that failure is what interviews and real apps hit. A Delete button with data-delete. On click, read the card id, filter it out of the array, then showAll again. The DOM matches the array.\n\nWhat this is\nA Delete button with data-delete. On click, read the card id, filter it out of the array, then showAll again. The DOM matches the array. Keep data in an array. Render that array into nodes. createElement + textContent is safe. One listener on the parent (delegation) hears every card, including new ones. preventDefault on submit. Walk the snippet like this: `cards = cards.filter((c) => c.id !== id` — Delete in data. `showAll(cards` — Delete on the page. `btn.dataset.delete = \"1\"` — mark as the delete control.\n\nWhat it solves\nAdd/delete/search stay in one paint function. Memory does not leak per-button listeners. User text cannot run as code. After this row you can demo the happy path and one failed path (empty input, wrong password, or missing id). A Delete button with data-delete. On click, read the card id, filter it out of the array, then showAll again. The DOM matches the array.\n\nReal-life example\nA pin board. The list of notices lives in a folder. You clear the board and pin each paper again. One teacher at the end of the table hears a tap on any tray. Map guest → user, counter → this function, back room → the array, file, or database in the snippet. delete a card.\n\nUses\nCard grids, todos, menus, vanilla JS before React. The same idea as mapping in React. On this sheet, the question is: Project: delete a card. Use the same idea in the next lab instead of copying a new pattern. Add/delete/search stay in one paint function. Memory does not leak per-button listeners. User text cannot run as code.\n\nWatch out\nDo not put user text in innerHTML. dataset.id must be the real id. Always replaceChildren before a full repaint or you duplicate. Read the comments on the right before you paste. If a line looks clever and has no comment, it is the line that will fail in an interview. A Delete button with data-delete. On click, read the card id, filter it out of the array, then showAll again. The DOM matches the array.",
      "code": "grid.addEventListener(\"click\", function (e) {\n  const btn = e.target.closest(\"[data-delete]\");\n  if (!btn) return;\n  const id = Number(btn.closest(\".card\").dataset.id);\n  cards = cards.filter((c) => c.id !== id);  // Delete in data\n  showAll(cards);  // Delete on the page\n});\n\nfunction makeCard(item) {\n  const card = document.createElement(\"article\");\n  card.className = \"card\";\n  card.dataset.id = String(item.id);\n  const btn = document.createElement(\"button\");\n  btn.type = \"button\";\n  btn.dataset.delete = \"1\";  // mark as the delete control\n  btn.textContent = \"Delete\";\n  card.append(btn);\n  return card;\n}",
      "codes": {
        "javascript": "grid.addEventListener(\"click\", function (e) {\n  const btn = e.target.closest(\"[data-delete]\");\n  if (!btn) return;\n  const id = Number(btn.closest(\".card\").dataset.id);\n  cards = cards.filter((c) => c.id !== id);  // Delete in data\n  showAll(cards);  // Delete on the page\n});\n\nfunction makeCard(item) {\n  const card = document.createElement(\"article\");\n  card.className = \"card\";\n  card.dataset.id = String(item.id);\n  const btn = document.createElement(\"button\");\n  btn.type = \"button\";\n  btn.dataset.delete = \"1\";  // mark as the delete control\n  btn.textContent = \"Delete\";\n  card.append(btn);\n  return card;\n}",
        "react": "function Grid({ cards, setCards }) {\n  function remove(id) {\n    setCards((list) => list.filter((c) => c.id !== id));  // Delete in state\n  }\n\n  return (\n    <div id=\"grid\">\n      {cards.map((item) => (\n        <article key={item.id} className=\"card\">\n          <button type=\"button\" onClick={() => remove(item.id)}>Delete</button>\n        </article>\n      ))}\n    </div>\n  );\n}"
      }
