      "q": "Project: open a modal when you click a card",
      "a": "Summary\nSummary: open a modal when you click a card. Click the card body (not Delete) → fill a dialog and showModal(). Store the id so Close knows which card you were viewing. This is a hands-on lab, not a riddle. You should be able to explain the function out loud, then type it from memory. What the code is doing: Walk the snippet like this: `if (e.target.closest(\"button\")) return` — let Delete / Like handle themselves. `dialog.showModal` — native modal. A line you can point at: const dialog = document.getElementById(\"modal\"); In an interview, start with the job in plain words (the first sentences here), then walk the snippet. Do not start with a shop story. When you are done: run it, break it with empty input or a wrong id, then fix the check. That is the whole point of a practice row. Add/delete/search stay in one paint function. Memory does not leak per-button listeners. User text cannot run as code.\n\nThe problem before\nPeople typed twenty cards in HTML. New cards did not get click listeners. innerHTML with user text became XSS. The page reloaded on every submit. This lab exists because that failure is what interviews and real apps hit. Click the card body (not Delete) → fill a dialog and showModal(). Store the id so Close knows which card you were viewing.\n\nWhat this is\nClick the card body (not Delete) → fill a dialog and showModal(). Store the id so Close knows which card you were viewing. Keep data in an array. Render that array into nodes. createElement + textContent is safe. One listener on the parent (delegation) hears every card, including new ones. preventDefault on submit. Walk the snippet like this: `if (e.target.closest(\"button\")) return` — let Delete / Like handle themselves. `dialog.showModal` — native modal.\n\nWhat it solves\nAdd/delete/search stay in one paint function. Memory does not leak per-button listeners. User text cannot run as code. After this row you can demo the happy path and one failed path (empty input, wrong password, or missing id). Click the card body (not Delete) → fill a dialog and showModal(). Store the id so Close knows which card you were viewing.\n\nReal-life example\nA pin board. The list of notices lives in a folder. You clear the board and pin each paper again. One teacher at the end of the table hears a tap on any tray. Map guest → user, counter → this function, back room → the array, file, or database in the snippet. open a modal when you click a card.\n\nUses\nCard grids, todos, menus, vanilla JS before React. The same idea as mapping in React. On this sheet, the question is: Project: open a modal when you click a card. Use the same idea in the next lab instead of copying a new pattern. Add/delete/search stay in one paint function. Memory does not leak per-button listeners. User text cannot run as code.\n\nWatch out\nDo not put user text in innerHTML. dataset.id must be the real id. Always replaceChildren before a full repaint or you duplicate. Read the comments on the right before you paste. If a line looks clever and has no comment, it is the line that will fail in an interview. Click the card body (not Delete) → fill a dialog and showModal(). Store the id so Close knows which card you were viewing.",
      "code": "const dialog = document.getElementById(\"modal\");\ngrid.addEventListener(\"click\", function (e) {\n  if (e.target.closest(\"button\")) return;  // let Delete / Like handle themselves\n  const card = e.target.closest(\".card\");\n  if (!card) return;\n  const item = cards.find((c) => c.id === Number(card.dataset.id));\n  dialog.querySelector(\"h2\").textContent = item.title;\n  dialog.querySelector(\"p\").textContent = item.text;\n  dialog.showModal();  // native modal\n});\ndialog.querySelector(\"[data-close]\").addEventListener(\"click\", function () {\n  dialog.close();\n});",
      "codes": {
        "javascript": "const dialog = document.getElementById(\"modal\");\ngrid.addEventListener(\"click\", function (e) {\n  if (e.target.closest(\"button\")) return;  // let Delete / Like handle themselves\n  const card = e.target.closest(\".card\");\n  if (!card) return;\n  const item = cards.find((c) => c.id === Number(card.dataset.id));\n  dialog.querySelector(\"h2\").textContent = item.title;\n  dialog.querySelector(\"p\").textContent = item.text;\n  dialog.showModal();  // native modal\n});\ndialog.querySelector(\"[data-close]\").addEventListener(\"click\", function () {\n  dialog.close();\n});",
        "react": "function App({ cards }) {\n  const [open, setOpen] = React.useState(null);  // which card, or null\n  const item = cards.find((c) => c.id === open);\n\n  return (\n    <>\n      <div id=\"grid\">\n        {cards.map((c) => (\n          <article key={c.id} className=\"card\" onClick={() => setOpen(c.id)}>\n            <h3>{c.title}</h3>\n          </article>\n        ))}\n      </div>\n      {item && (\n        <dialog open>\n          <h2>{item.title}</h2>\n          <p>{item.text}</p>\n          <button type=\"button\" onClick={() => setOpen(null)}>Close</button>\n        </dialog>\n      )}\n    </>\n  );\n}"
      }
