      "q": "Why not put the whole card in innerHTML?",
      "a": "Summary\nSummary: Why not put the whole card in innerHTML?. innerHTML parses HTML. If title is <img onerror=...> you just ran attacker JS (XSS). textContent and createElement treat it as letters. Template strings are fine for your own fixed markup, not for user strings. This is a hands-on lab, not a riddle. You should be able to explain the function out loud, then type it from memory. What the code is doing: Walk the snippet like this: `title.textContent = item.title` — safe. card.innerHTML = \"<h3>\" + item.title + \"</h3>\";  // XSS if title is dirty. A line you can point at: title.textContent = item.title;  // safe In an interview, start with the job in plain words (the first sentences here), then walk the snippet. Do not start with a shop story. When you are done: run it, break it with empty input or a wrong id, then fix the check. That is the whole point of a practice row. Guest handwriting stays handwriting. <script> shows as letters. Interviewers want the three kinds and one sink: innerHTML.\n\nThe problem before\nA todo title contained a script tag. innerHTML ran it as your site's JavaScript. Tokens leaked. People thought they were 'just showing a name'. This lab exists because that failure is what interviews and real apps hit. innerHTML parses HTML. If title is <img onerror=...> you just ran attacker JS (XSS). textContent and createElement treat it as letters. Template strings are fine for your own fixed markup, not for user strings.\n\nWhat this is\ninnerHTML parses HTML. If title is <img onerror=...> you just ran attacker JS (XSS). textContent and createElement treat it as letters. Template strings are fine for your own fixed markup, not for user strings. XSS means attacker text runs as JS in your page. Use textContent or a framework that escapes. Stored XSS sits in the database. Reflected XSS rides a query string. DOM XSS writes location.hash into HTML. Walk the snippet like this: `title.textContent = item.title` — safe. card.innerHTML = \"<h3>\" + item.title + \"</h3>\";  // XSS if title is dirty.\n\nWhat it solves\nGuest handwriting stays handwriting. <script> shows as letters. Interviewers want the three kinds and one sink: innerHTML. After this row you can demo the happy path and one failed path (empty input, wrong password, or missing id). innerHTML parses HTML. If title is <img onerror=...> you just ran attacker JS (XSS). textContent and createElement treat it as letters. Template strings are fine for your own fixed markup, not for user strings.\n\nReal-life example\nA suggestion box. If the monitor reads the note aloud as an order, the till opens. If they pin the paper as text, it is only ugly ink. textContent pins the paper. Map guest → user, counter → this function, back room → the array, file, or database in the snippet. Why not put the whole card in innerHTML?.\n\nUses\nAny user title, comment, search, markdown. React children are safe; dangerouslySetInnerHTML is not. On this sheet, the question is: Why not put the whole card in innerHTML?. Use the same idea in the next lab instead of copying a new pattern. Guest handwriting stays handwriting. <script> shows as letters. Interviewers want the three kinds and one sink: innerHTML.\n\nWatch out\nEscaping in the database is not enough if you later concat HTML. href and onclick are sinks too. Pair with CSP. Read the comments on the right before you paste. If a line looks clever and has no comment, it is the line that will fail in an interview. innerHTML parses HTML. If title is <img onerror=...> you just ran attacker JS (XSS). textContent and createElement treat it as letters. Template strings are fine for your own fixed markup, not for user strings.",
      "code": "title.textContent = item.title;  // safe\n// card.innerHTML = \"<h3>\" + item.title + \"</h3>\";  // XSS if title is dirty",
